    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1095">

                <pb facs="1255" n="1111" xml:id="pg1111"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">662.11241/47</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Germany</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Schurman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-12-09">December 9, 1929</date>.<lb/>[Received December
                        27.]</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 5142</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: In continuation of my despatch No. 4504,
                    dated April 29, 1929, I have the honor to enclose for the Department’s
                    information copy in translation of a decree<anchor n="92" xml:id="d1095-f1"/>
                    granting exemption for the payment of customs duties and internal taxes to
                    foreign consular officers stationed in Germany, provided reciprocity is granted
                    by the appointing State, transmitted to the Embassy by the German Foreign
                    Office.</p>
                <p>With reference to the Department’s telegraphic instruction No. 25, dated April
                    12, 3 p.m., 1926, authorizing the Embassy to insist on full reciprocity for
                    American consular officers assigned to Germany in importing effects and
                    merchandise free of duty at any time, the Embassy has the honor to request the
                    Department’s authorization to inform the Foreign Office that the reciprocity is
                    granted to German consular officers in the United States and that the
                    authorization be sent by cablegram in order to assist American consular officers
                    in Germany in receiving their Christmas packages.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Jacob Gould
                    Schurman</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
